From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 17:02:14 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] refactor(tsf): the data provider takes the composition, not a
 renderer

IDataProvider::GetRenderer() made TSF depend on one particular terminal engine.
Implementation reached through it and wrote the in-progress composition into
that renderer's data itself, dereferencing it unchecked - so a provider with no
such renderer could not merely return null, and a ghostty pane had to be kept
away from TSF entirely. That is why it had no IME at all.

SetComposition/ClearComposition replace it. Where the preedit goes is now the
provider's business: cascadia writes it into its renderer exactly as before
(the body moved, unchanged, including the lock and the repaint), and a ghostty
pane hands it to ghostty_surface_preedit, which draws its own.

Three implementors, not one. TermControl's is the interesting one; HwndTerminal
and conhost's WindowProc have their own, and conhost's is not built by the
`terminal` project - only the package build caught it. Both got the same body
they were already getting by proxy.

Two more things a ghostty pane needed, both of which were asking cascadia's core
for something that is on IControlCore: GetCursorPosition, so the candidate
window opens at the cursor rather than the corner of the screen, and
HandleOutput, which would otherwise have composed text and then swallowed it.

This also removes the seventh and last get_self escape ADR 0001 enumerated - TSF
no longer needs WT's Renderer at all. _tsfSupported() stays, returning true: an
engine with no preedit path of its own would still have to say so, and saying it
in one place is what kept the ghostty pane from crashing on TSF while it could
not.

**Unverified.** Nothing here proves an IME works - that needs an installed CJK
IME and a human, and none is installed on this machine. What is verified is that
cascadia's path is byte-for-byte the same work in a different place, and that
all four suites pass: terminalCore 54/54, unitSettingsModel 159/159,
unitControl 62/62, terminalApp 51/51.

